WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.235
Negotiations between Hollywood studios

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.270
and the Writers Guild are expected

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.572
to resume next week,

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.741
according to multiple reports.

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:11.277
The strike is now almost four months

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.080
old and talks broke off in mid-August.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.915
And it comes as both Drew

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:18.218
Barrymore and Bill Maher

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:19.352
announced this week

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:20.754
that their talk shows

00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:23.423
would resume production without writers.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:25.325
They join several other talk

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:27.460
shows that have been airing new episodes,

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.362
including The View and Live

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.697
with Kelly and Mark.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.165
The writers went on strike

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:33.033
back in May,

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:34.401
demanding better pay

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:35.201
and a change

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.170
to the compensation structure

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.671
as the industry moves toward

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.407
a streaming model and away

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:42.442
from traditional broadcasting.

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:44.577
The Actors Guild went on strike

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:45.845
two months later over

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.147
many of the same issues,

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.082
but they also won an agreement

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:50.417
that protects actors

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:51.951
against some of the uses

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.486
of artificial intelligence

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:55.455
in film and television.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.323
I want to bring in Michael Jackman.

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:58.825
He is a television

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.493
comedy writer and showrunner

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.162
who has worked on shows

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.863
such as King of the Hill,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.466
Rules of Engagement, and Tacoma, FDR,

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:08.068
among others.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:09.269
Good to see you.

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:09.602
Michael.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.939
So are you any more optimistic today

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:14.441
than, I don't know,

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:15.475
a couple of months ago

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:16.843
when all of this got underway

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:17.310
that perhaps

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.312
negotiations can get underway

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.947
and there could be

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.115
some sort of deal struck?

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:25.585
As a professional sitcom writer.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:25.985
I'm trying

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.555
not to be optimistic about anything.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.223
We get our hopes up all the time.

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:31.024
I really hope

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.592
that we can get a much better

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.560
offer from the studios

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.061
because we all want to get to work,

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:35.528
all of it.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:37.130
You know, I'm in my garage

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.965
and I mentioned I'm in my garage now.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:40.400
I could be sitting on a yoga mall

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.801
if I had to be so long.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.236
I want to be back at work.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:43.803
Yeah.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.839
And can you kind of paint a picture?

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:49.576
What are a lot of writers doing right now

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:52.745
to make ends meet, to pay the bills,

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:54.114
you know, well,

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:56.015
while they are on the picket line,

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:58.685
or at least supporting what's happening

00:01:59.752 --> 00:01:59.986
Yeah.

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:00.620
I mean, that

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:01.287
that's the thing

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.022
like when I first broke into the business

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.157
you could make this is

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.892
I don't want to say what 1990.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.161
You can make a living full time

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:09.729
as a comedy writer on a show.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:09.963
You know,

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:10.797
if you're working,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:11.998
let's say ten months out of the year,

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:13.433
you take a hiatus and go back to work

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:14.200
and it was.

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.469
But now if you're on a hit show

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:17.303
a really a hit show

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.171
you might only work

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.639
eight or do eight

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:21.207
or ten episodes in a season.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.176
And so then how do you get

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.043
how do you get by.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.879
Sometimes your contract won't like it

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.113
sometimes you can scramble

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.114
and pick up some side

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.183
works out, sell a pilot or

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.918
there's a lot of hustle.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.487
Back in 2008 when the last strike was,

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:35.588
I remember

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:36.856
the leverage from the studio was,

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.158
what can you guys really afford

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.392
to be out of work for three months?

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.261
And we're like, No, not really.

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:43.363
But now they've lost that leverage.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.031
Can you guys afford to be out of work

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:45.632
for once?

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:46.900
We're already out of work

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.601
nine months out of the year.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:49.802
You lost that leverage

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.404
when you turned our Japanese department

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.239
professional. So.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.775
So, you know, I teach courses, stuff

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.810
where I have a book coming at

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.412
whatever you can do to hustle.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.313
Yeah,

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.382
but it's been hard for a lot of people

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:03.349
because some people are having

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.285
to sell their assets because,

00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:05.652
you know,

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:07.153
they they cannot

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.087
they can no longer

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.522
make ends meet in a while.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.090
And while this is happening,

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.194
I do want to play a clip that you posted

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.429
back in July, if we could.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.398
Let's do that. Yeah.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:19.265
Today

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.601
I am picketing the surface of the sun.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:23.336
I don't know what to send it

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:25.672
to the writers, but apparently something

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.673
you know, I don't ask questions.

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:28.174
So I just show up, you know,

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.075
4000 degrees.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:30.543
I tell you what,

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.412
back when it was just the writers

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:34.080
and the picket line,

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:35.648
the place stunk of sunscreen.

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.217
But now that the actors have joined

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:38.051
us, right now,

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:39.052
it stinks of cocoa

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:41.621
butter and lemon juice cleanse.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.623
So a little tongue in cheek there.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:44.357
But, you know,

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:45.491
so when you were doing that in

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:47.527
July, did you.

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:48.228
Were you a little bit

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:48.962
more hopeful

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:51.197
that perhaps everyone will be

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:53.166
would have been back to work by now

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:56.302
with an agreement in place

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:57.537
and you know,

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:58.338
that it would be

00:03:58.338 --> 00:03:59.505
a totally different scenario

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:00.974
than what it is now.

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:02.942
You all right? You know what?

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.644
When the actress joined us in the strike,

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:05.778
it definitely helped it

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.280
because their high profile

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:07.680
you know,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.282
they're higher profile than writers.

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.017
So it definitely added a little weight,

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:12.785
a little more power to the cause.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:14.220
So was I hopeful?

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.188
Yeah, a little.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.557
Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:20.360
And then, you know, as we mentioned,

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:20.893
you know,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.830
some shows are getting back on the air

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.231
next week.

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:27.066
The view has been on.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:28.368
They make mention of the fact

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:29.235
that they have been doing

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.403
so without their writers,

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:31.537
their handwriting,

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:32.972
you know, their own material.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.808
Drew Barrymore,

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.077
her show is returning to production,

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:41.414
but she's been taking a lot of heat

00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:44.817
for, you know, getting back to business,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:45.785
even though she says it's

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:49.522
going to be in compliance with the strike

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:51.858
and that they won't have writers.

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:54.861
Maybe you can explain to us,

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:55.395
you know what,

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.930
why she is getting so much heat.

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:01.601
Why are circumstances different for her?

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:02.835
Are they?

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:04.404
I don't know. I don't know if they are.

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:06.639
I think I think everyone's taking heat

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.274
I think maybe in Drew's case,

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:10.176
she's such a beloved personality.

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:12.245
Like she's she's charming,

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:13.079
she's endearing.

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:14.647
She seems very empathetic.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.749
And so it's not like I don't think,

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:18.017
oh, I'm not angry

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:19.185
and I'm just kind of disappointed

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.087
because it actually hurts.

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:21.754
The more weight,

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:22.789
the more pressure

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:23.523
you can put on the studios

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:24.223
to make a deal,

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:25.825
the faster this comes to resolution

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.027
by putting your show back on,

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:29.862
you take that pressure off.

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:30.630
And so is

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.231
it just disappointing in the sense that,

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:35.802
you know, we all love her, you know, that

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.104
when your kids do something wrong,

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:39.505
you're just disappointed.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.140
But when my neighbors something wrong,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.442
same thing. I'm not disappointed.

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:43.443
I don't really care because I don't

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.377
you know, I don't love that.

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:45.211
Are you disappointed?

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:46.746
In the other shows, too?

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:48.481
Because, you know,

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:51.284
I guess everyone from set design,

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:54.854
you know, to catering,

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.023
there are a lot of jobs involved

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.792
in getting all those shows on the air.

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:02.028
And, you know,

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.496
perhaps a lot of those shows are saying

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:05.431
we want as many people as possible

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:06.332
to get back to work,

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:07.600
even though we know

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.535
many of the writers cannot.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.771
So is that something

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:13.539
that's taken into consideration

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.375
or is it an issue of,

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:16.242
like you just said,

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:17.944
to make the strike strong,

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:20.446
everyone has to be on one accord

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.148
in terms of, you know,

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:23.583
the Actors and Writers Guild

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:27.019
a part of what's at stake is

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.055
so if shows in the future,

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:30.356
and I think this is what their goal is in

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:30.656
the near

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:32.158
future, is have shows written

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:34.394
and performed inside the computer

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:35.828
and when that's the case,

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.029
there is no need for a hair,

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:39.599
makeup, wardrobe set, camera operators.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:40.833
All those jobs go away

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:42.402
when the show is made

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:43.669
inside the computer.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:45.238
So this is a fight.

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:46.906
This is everyone's fight. It's coming.

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:48.541
And so I think we should first

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:50.109
take the stand on it.

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:50.877
All right.

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:51.944
Michael Jackman.

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.312
Glad you could be with us.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:54.380
Thanks so much.

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.816
Perhaps we'll keep tabs with you because.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.418
Sure, everybody,

00:06:58.418 --> 00:06:58.785
you know,

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:01.120
wants some resolution, but

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.688
you're you're helping

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:04.590
helping us to kind of

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.191
get a sense

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:05.591
as to what

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.026
everybody is going through

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:08.728
on a day to day basis.

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:10.563
Hopefully we'll have you back.

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:11.864
Thank you so much. I'd love to.

